{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-336533" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A series RL circuit has a source electromotive force (EMF) of $6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistance of $18\\,Ω,$ and an inductance of $3\\,\\text{H}.$ Find the differential equation governing the amount of current $I$ (in amperes) in the circuit at time $t$ (in seconds)." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043558/state.json" "file_sha256" "07640b47d53face31c80b92b74378b0b8702b2a2da365787630a579bc80f68d3"} {"question" "q-336533" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Kirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RL circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=18\\,Ω.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=18I\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=3\\,\\text{H}.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=3\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of current $I$ in the circuit is\n\n$$\n6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)=18I+3\\,\\frac{dI}{dt},\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dI}{dt}+6I=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043558/state.json" "file_sha256" "07640b47d53face31c80b92b74378b0b8702b2a2da365787630a579bc80f68d3"} {"question" "q-336533" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043558/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b33375559aab7df667a4dcfaab352b9217cd7d366be04f7ee1dc7912e274f5ad"} {"question" "q-336533" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043558/state.json" "file_sha256" "07640b47d53face31c80b92b74378b0b8702b2a2da365787630a579bc80f68d3"} {"question" "q-336533" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{dI}{dt}+18I=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)"] ["math" "\\frac{dI}{dt}+4I=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)"] ["math" "\\frac{dI}{dt}+6I=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)"] ["math" "\\frac{dI}{dt}-18I=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)"] ["math" "\\frac{dI}{dt}-6I=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043558/state.json" "file_sha256" "07640b47d53face31c80b92b74378b0b8702b2a2da365787630a579bc80f68d3"} {"question" "q-336533" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{dI}{dt}+6I=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043558/state.json" "file_sha256" "07640b47d53face31c80b92b74378b0b8702b2a2da365787630a579bc80f68d3"} {"question" "q-336546" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043558/assets/999acaa0bc014b7afc7c916dd03c957e15707ca680836341e5a071a1f321e97d.png)\n\nFind the differential equation governing the amount of current $I$ (in amperes) at time $t$ (in seconds) in the circuit given in the diagram above." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043558/state.json" "file_sha256" "07640b47d53face31c80b92b74378b0b8702b2a2da365787630a579bc80f68d3"} {"question" "q-336546" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The diagram shows a series RL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $E(t)=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor with resistance $9\\,Ω,$ and an inductor with an inductance of $3\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RL circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=9\\,Ω.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=9I\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=3\\,\\text{H}.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=3\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of current $I$ in the circuit is\n\n$$\n3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)=9I+3\\,\\frac{dI}{dt},\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dI}{dt}+3I=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043558/state.json" "file_sha256" "07640b47d53face31c80b92b74378b0b8702b2a2da365787630a579bc80f68d3"} {"question" "q-336546" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043558/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b33375559aab7df667a4dcfaab352b9217cd7d366be04f7ee1dc7912e274f5ad"} {"question" "q-336546" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043558/state.json" "file_sha256" "07640b47d53face31c80b92b74378b0b8702b2a2da365787630a579bc80f68d3"} {"question" "q-336546" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{dI}{dt}+3I=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)"] ["math" "\\frac{dI}{dt}+3I=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)"] ["math" "\\frac{dI}{dt}+9I=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)"] ["math" "\\frac{dI}{dt}-3I=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)"] ["math" "\\frac{dI}{dt}-9I=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043558/state.json" "file_sha256" "07640b47d53face31c80b92b74378b0b8702b2a2da365787630a579bc80f68d3"} {"question" "q-336546" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{dI}{dt}+3I=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043558/state.json" "file_sha256" "07640b47d53face31c80b92b74378b0b8702b2a2da365787630a579bc80f68d3"} {"question" "q-336542" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043558/assets/91c132c39e9dedeb5377d850a344e555ba0755c3fd6183220674cb7f967e943b.png)\n\nFind the differential equation governing the amount of current $I$ (in amperes) at time $t$ (in seconds) in the circuit given in the diagram above." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043558/state.json" "file_sha256" "07640b47d53face31c80b92b74378b0b8702b2a2da365787630a579bc80f68d3"} {"question" "q-336542" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The diagram shows a series RL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $6\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor with resistance $18\\,Ω,$ and an inductor with an inductance of $3\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RL circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=18\\,Ω.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=18I\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=3\\,\\text{H}.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=3\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of current $I$ in the circuit is\n\n$$\n6=18I+3\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dI}{dt}+6I=2.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043558/state.json" "file_sha256" "07640b47d53face31c80b92b74378b0b8702b2a2da365787630a579bc80f68d3"} {"question" "q-336542" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043558/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b33375559aab7df667a4dcfaab352b9217cd7d366be04f7ee1dc7912e274f5ad"} {"question" "q-336542" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043558/state.json" "file_sha256" "07640b47d53face31c80b92b74378b0b8702b2a2da365787630a579bc80f68d3"} {"question" "q-336542" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{dI}{dt}+18I=2"] ["math" "\\frac{dI}{dt}+6I=2"] ["math" "\\frac{dI}{dt}+6I=6"] ["math" "\\frac{dI}{dt}-18I=6"] ["math" "\\frac{dI}{dt}-6I=2"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043558/state.json" "file_sha256" "07640b47d53face31c80b92b74378b0b8702b2a2da365787630a579bc80f68d3"} {"question" "q-336542" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{dI}{dt}+6I=2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043558/state.json" "file_sha256" "07640b47d53face31c80b92b74378b0b8702b2a2da365787630a579bc80f68d3"} {"question" "q-336543" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043558/assets/a7005971c36efa45c4e8c3f752bdc4aec5af3a8b92734e83e85470d10bfbc299.png)\n\nFind the differential equation governing the amount of current $I$ (in amperes) at time $t$ (in seconds) in the circuit given in the diagram above." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043558/state.json" "file_sha256" "07640b47d53face31c80b92b74378b0b8702b2a2da365787630a579bc80f68d3"} {"question" "q-336543" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The diagram shows a series RL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $8\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor with resistance $16\\,Ω,$ and an inductor with an inductance of $4\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RL circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=16\\,Ω.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=16I\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=4\\,\\text{H}.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=4\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of current $I$ in the circuit is\n\n$$\n8=16I+4\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dI}{dt}+4I=2.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043558/state.json" "file_sha256" "07640b47d53face31c80b92b74378b0b8702b2a2da365787630a579bc80f68d3"} {"question" "q-336543" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043558/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b33375559aab7df667a4dcfaab352b9217cd7d366be04f7ee1dc7912e274f5ad"} {"question" "q-336543" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043558/state.json" "file_sha256" "07640b47d53face31c80b92b74378b0b8702b2a2da365787630a579bc80f68d3"} {"question" "q-336543" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{dI}{dt}+16I=2"] ["math" "\\frac{dI}{dt}+4I=2"] ["math" "\\frac{dI}{dt}+4I=8"] ["math" "\\frac{dI}{dt}-16I=8"] ["math" "\\frac{dI}{dt}-4I=2"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043558/state.json" "file_sha256" "07640b47d53face31c80b92b74378b0b8702b2a2da365787630a579bc80f68d3"} {"question" "q-336543" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{dI}{dt}+4I=2"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043558/state.json" "file_sha256" "07640b47d53face31c80b92b74378b0b8702b2a2da365787630a579bc80f68d3"} {"question" "q-336534" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043558/assets/c644931a5d7c3b015e00518269920718cde39882b45d3589e12e6b90d0391d46.png)\n\nFind the differential equation governing the amount of current $I$ (in amperes) at time $t$ (in seconds) in the circuit given in the diagram above." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043558/state.json" "file_sha256" "07640b47d53face31c80b92b74378b0b8702b2a2da365787630a579bc80f68d3"} {"question" "q-336534" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The diagram shows a series RL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $9\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor with resistance $36\\,Ω,$ and an inductor with an inductance of $3\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RL circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=36\\,Ω.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=36I\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=3\\,\\text{H}.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=3\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of current $I$ in the circuit is\n\n$$\n9=36I+3\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dI}{dt}+12I=3.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043558/state.json" "file_sha256" "07640b47d53face31c80b92b74378b0b8702b2a2da365787630a579bc80f68d3"} {"question" "q-336534" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043558/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b33375559aab7df667a4dcfaab352b9217cd7d366be04f7ee1dc7912e274f5ad"} {"question" "q-336534" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043558/state.json" "file_sha256" "07640b47d53face31c80b92b74378b0b8702b2a2da365787630a579bc80f68d3"} {"question" "q-336534" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{dI}{dt}+12I=3"] ["math" "\\frac{dI}{dt}+12I=9"] ["math" "\\frac{dI}{dt}+36I=3"] ["math" "\\frac{dI}{dt}-12I=3"] ["math" "\\frac{dI}{dt}-36I=9"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043558/state.json" "file_sha256" "07640b47d53face31c80b92b74378b0b8702b2a2da365787630a579bc80f68d3"} {"question" "q-336534" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{dI}{dt}+12I=3"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043558/state.json" "file_sha256" "07640b47d53face31c80b92b74378b0b8702b2a2da365787630a579bc80f68d3"} {"question" "q-336643" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The current $I(t),$ in amperes, in a series RL circuit is governed by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dI}{dt}+6I=3,\\,I(0)=1.\n$$\n\nFind the steady-state current for this circuit.\n\nThe steady-state current is\n{{field-1}} amperes." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043558/state.json" "file_sha256" "07640b47d53face31c80b92b74378b0b8702b2a2da365787630a579bc80f68d3"} {"question" "q-336643" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The equation is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${I}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{I}_{c}}{dt}+6{I}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{I}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-6t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${I}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{I}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0,$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0+6\\alpha =3\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is ${I}_{p}(t)=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t)={I}_{c}(t)+{I}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-6t}+\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${I}_{c}=A{e}^{-6t}$ is the *transient current*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The constant ${I}_{p}=\\frac{1}{2}$ is the *steady-state current*. As $t\\to \\infty ,$ the current $I$ approaches the value of the steady-state current.\n\nTherefore, the steady-state current is $\\frac{1}{2}$ amperes." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043558/state.json" "file_sha256" "07640b47d53face31c80b92b74378b0b8702b2a2da365787630a579bc80f68d3"} {"question" "q-336643" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043558/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b33375559aab7df667a4dcfaab352b9217cd7d366be04f7ee1dc7912e274f5ad"} {"question" "q-336643" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043558/state.json" "file_sha256" "07640b47d53face31c80b92b74378b0b8702b2a2da365787630a579bc80f68d3"} {"question" "q-336643" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{2}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043558/state.json" "file_sha256" "07640b47d53face31c80b92b74378b0b8702b2a2da365787630a579bc80f68d3"} {"question" "q-336645" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The current $I(t),$ in amperes, in a series RL circuit is governed by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dI}{dt}+8I=5,\\,I(0)=3.\n$$\n\nFind the steady-state current for this circuit.\n\nThe steady-state current is\n{{field-1}} amperes." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043558/state.json" "file_sha256" "07640b47d53face31c80b92b74378b0b8702b2a2da365787630a579bc80f68d3"} {"question" "q-336645" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The equation is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${I}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{I}_{c}}{dt}+8{I}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{I}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-8t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${I}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{I}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0,$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0+8\\alpha =5\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =\\frac{5}{8}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is ${I}_{p}(t)=\\frac{5}{8}.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t)={I}_{c}(t)+{I}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-8t}+\\frac{5}{8}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${I}_{c}=A{e}^{-8t}$ is the *transient current*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The constant ${I}_{p}=\\frac{5}{8}$ is the *steady-state current*. As $t\\to \\infty ,$ the current $I$ approaches the value of the steady-state current.\n\nTherefore, the steady-state current is $\\frac{5}{8}$ amperes." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043558/state.json" "file_sha256" "07640b47d53face31c80b92b74378b0b8702b2a2da365787630a579bc80f68d3"} {"question" "q-336645" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043558/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b33375559aab7df667a4dcfaab352b9217cd7d366be04f7ee1dc7912e274f5ad"} {"question" "q-336645" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043558/state.json" "file_sha256" "07640b47d53face31c80b92b74378b0b8702b2a2da365787630a579bc80f68d3"} {"question" "q-336645" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{5}{8}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043558/state.json" "file_sha256" "07640b47d53face31c80b92b74378b0b8702b2a2da365787630a579bc80f68d3"} {"question" "q-336651" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043558/assets/62dc1d019806908359e3956386f40c1a3ca53d720db7ba0c3122d8eb4ba37c2e.png)\n\nFind an equation for the current $I(t)$ (in amperes) in the circuit given in the diagram above, expressing your answer in terms of time $t$ (in seconds) **only**, given that the current when $t=0\\,\\text{s}$ is $\\frac{3}{2}\\,\\text{A}.$\n\n$I(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043558/state.json" "file_sha256" "07640b47d53face31c80b92b74378b0b8702b2a2da365787630a579bc80f68d3"} {"question" "q-336651" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The diagram shows a series RL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $8\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance of $16\\,Ω,$ and an inductor of inductance $2\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RL circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=16\\,Ω.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=16I\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=2\\,\\text{H}.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=2\\cdot \\,\\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of current $I$ in the circuit is\n\n$$\n8=16I+2\\cdot \\,\\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dI}{dt}+8I=4.\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${I}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{I}_{c}}{dt}+8{I}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{I}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-8t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${I}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{I}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0+8\\alpha =4\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is ${I}_{p}(t)=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t)={I}_{c}(t)+{I}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-8t}+\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the circuit starts with an initial current of $\\frac{3}{2}\\,\\text{A}.$ So, we can apply the initial condition $I(0)=\\frac{3}{2},$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(0) & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ A{e}^{-8(0)}+\\frac{1}{2} & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ A & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $I(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{-8t}+\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${I}_{c}={e}^{-8t}$ is the *transient current*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The constant ${I}_{p}=\\frac{1}{2}$ is the *steady-state current*. As $t\\to \\infty ,$ the current $I$ approaches the value of the steady-state current." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043558/state.json" "file_sha256" "07640b47d53face31c80b92b74378b0b8702b2a2da365787630a579bc80f68d3"} {"question" "q-336651" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043558/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b33375559aab7df667a4dcfaab352b9217cd7d366be04f7ee1dc7912e274f5ad"} {"question" "q-336651" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043558/state.json" "file_sha256" "07640b47d53face31c80b92b74378b0b8702b2a2da365787630a579bc80f68d3"} {"question" "q-336651" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "e^{-8t}+\\frac{1}{2}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043558/state.json" "file_sha256" "07640b47d53face31c80b92b74378b0b8702b2a2da365787630a579bc80f68d3"} {"question" "q-336653" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A series RL circuit has a source electromotive force (EMF) of $4\\,\\text{V},$ a resistance of $16\\,Ω,$ and an inductance of $2\\,\\text{H}.$\nFind, **rounded to two decimal places**, the circuit's current after $0.03$ seconds given that the initial current is $3\\,\\text{A}.$\n\nThe current after $0.03$ seconds is\n{{field-1}} amperes." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043558/state.json" "file_sha256" "07640b47d53face31c80b92b74378b0b8702b2a2da365787630a579bc80f68d3"} {"question" "q-336653" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Kirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RL circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=16\\,Ω.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=16I\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=2\\,\\text{H}.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=2\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of current $I$ in the circuit is\n\n$$\n4=16I+2\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dI}{dt}+8I=2.\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${I}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{I}_{c}}{dt}+8{I}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{I}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-8t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${I}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{I}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0,$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0+8\\alpha =2\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =\\frac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is ${I}_{p}(t)=\\frac{1}{4}.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t)={I}_{c}(t)+{I}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-8t}+\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the circuit starts with an initial current of $3\\,\\text{A}.$ So, we can apply the initial condition $I(0)=3,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(0) & =3 \\\\ A{e}^{-8(0)}+\\frac{1}{4} & =3 \\\\ A & =\\frac{11}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $I(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & =\\frac{11}{4}{e}^{-8t}+\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(11{e}^{-8t}+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the current in the circuit after $t=0.03$ seconds is\n\n$$\nI(0.03)=\\frac{1}{4}(11{e}^{-8(0.03)}+1)≈2.41\\,\\text{A},\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043558/state.json" "file_sha256" "07640b47d53face31c80b92b74378b0b8702b2a2da365787630a579bc80f68d3"} {"question" "q-336653" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043558/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b33375559aab7df667a4dcfaab352b9217cd7d366be04f7ee1dc7912e274f5ad"} {"question" "q-336653" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043558/state.json" "file_sha256" "07640b47d53face31c80b92b74378b0b8702b2a2da365787630a579bc80f68d3"} {"question" "q-336653" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2.41"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043558/state.json" "file_sha256" "07640b47d53face31c80b92b74378b0b8702b2a2da365787630a579bc80f68d3"} {"question" "q-336649" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043558/assets/7e7a2252fdab7a363557a056c58b10f228a13b4aa2029b6b20867a95e5cdde79.png)\n\nFind an equation for the current $I(t)$ (in amperes) in the circuit given in the diagram above, expressing your answer in terms of time $t$ (in seconds) **only**, given that the current when $t=0\\,\\text{s}$ is $1\\,\\text{A}.$\n\n$I(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043558/state.json" "file_sha256" "07640b47d53face31c80b92b74378b0b8702b2a2da365787630a579bc80f68d3"} {"question" "q-336649" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The diagram shows a series RL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $5\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $25\\,Ω,$ and an inductor of inductance $1\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RL circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=25\\,Ω.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=25I\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=1\\,\\text{H}.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=\\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of current $I$ in the circuit is\n\n$$\n5=25I+\\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dI}{dt}+25I=5.\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${I}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{I}_{c}}{dt}+25{I}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{I}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-25t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n${I}_{p}=\\alpha .$ \nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${I}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{I}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n \n\n$$\n0+25\\alpha =5\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =\\frac{1}{5}\n$$\n\n  Therefore, the particular solution is ${I}_{p}(t)=\\frac{1}{5}.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t)={I}_{c}(t)+{I}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-25t}+\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the circuit starts with an initial current of $1\\,\\text{A}.$ So, we can apply the initial condition $I(0)=1,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(0) & =1 \\\\ A{e}^{-25(0)}+\\frac{1}{5} & =1 \\\\ A & =\\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $I(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & =\\frac{4}{5}{e}^{-25t}+\\frac{1}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(4{e}^{-25t}+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${I}_{c}=\\frac{4}{5}{e}^{-25t}$ is the *transient current*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The constant ${I}_{p}=\\frac{1}{5}$ is the *steady-state current*. As $t\\to \\infty ,$ the current $I$ approaches the value of the steady-state current." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043558/state.json" "file_sha256" "07640b47d53face31c80b92b74378b0b8702b2a2da365787630a579bc80f68d3"} {"question" "q-336649" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043558/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b33375559aab7df667a4dcfaab352b9217cd7d366be04f7ee1dc7912e274f5ad"} {"question" "q-336649" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043558/state.json" "file_sha256" "07640b47d53face31c80b92b74378b0b8702b2a2da365787630a579bc80f68d3"} {"question" "q-336649" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{4}{5}e^{-25t}+\\frac{1}{5}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043558/state.json" "file_sha256" "07640b47d53face31c80b92b74378b0b8702b2a2da365787630a579bc80f68d3"} {"question" "q-336740" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043558/assets/7f997f7647886e1eea24d292ddd66b2d331d6f7d4c7ffe47b6a52b52dd029a44.png)\n\nConsider the RL circuit shown above. Given that the current $I(t)$ at time $t$ seconds is given by\n\n$$\nI(t)=A{e}^{-6t}+\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t),\n$$\n\nwhere $A,$ $\\alpha ,$ and $\\beta $ are constants, find the value of $\\alpha +\\beta .$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043558/state.json" "file_sha256" "07640b47d53face31c80b92b74378b0b8702b2a2da365787630a579bc80f68d3"} {"question" "q-336740" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The diagram shows a series RL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $12\\,Ω,$ and an inductor of inductance $2\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RL circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=12\\,Ω.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=12I\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=2\\,\\text{H}.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=2\\cdot \\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of current $I$ in the circuit is\n\n$$\n4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)=12I+2\\cdot \\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dI}{dt}+6I=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${I}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{I}_{c}}{dt}+6{I}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{I}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-6t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $3t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal with argument $3t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t), \\\\ \\frac{d{I}_{p}}{dt} & =-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t))+6(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ (-3\\alpha +6\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+(6\\alpha +3\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-3\\alpha +6\\beta =2\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) \\\\ 6\\alpha +3\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{2}{15},$ $\\beta =\\frac{4}{15}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{2}{15}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{4}{15}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t)={I}_{c}(t)+{I}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-6t}-\\frac{2}{15}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{4}{15}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\alpha =-\\frac{2}{15},\\,\\beta =\\frac{4}{15}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\alpha +\\beta  & =-\\frac{2}{15}+\\frac{4}{15} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043558/state.json" "file_sha256" "07640b47d53face31c80b92b74378b0b8702b2a2da365787630a579bc80f68d3"} {"question" "q-336740" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043558/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b33375559aab7df667a4dcfaab352b9217cd7d366be04f7ee1dc7912e274f5ad"} {"question" "q-336740" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043558/state.json" "file_sha256" "07640b47d53face31c80b92b74378b0b8702b2a2da365787630a